WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.568
-Hey!

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.735
-Hi, Jimmy.

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.938
-Oh, thank you so much
for doing our show.

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.106
I appreciate it.

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:07.907
You know, I'm the biggest fan of

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.109
"Sisterhood of
the Traveling Pants,"

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:10.777
so I have to talk about it.

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.445
And I know I'm not
your audience,

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.180
but I loved it so much.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:17.550
I know you guys are actually
pretty close in real life.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.286
Right?
-We are. We totally are.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:23.189
-I've seen photos of you guys
out. I'm like, "What?"

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.892
Like, I would probably freak o--
I might cry

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.961
if I ever saw the whole --
you guys all together.

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:30.629
-[ Laughs ] Well, let's make
that happen, Jimmy.

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:31.731
I want to see you cry.
-No, don't make me cry.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.032
But I do love you guys.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.502
-Well, there was one time
where -- well, they surprised me

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:38.671
with a bachelorette party
that I didn't want.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.974
But they took me to
a restaurant, and, of course,

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:43.976
at each of our place was, like,
a pair of jeans

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:45.612
and arts and crafts.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:49.816
So that night, like,
restaurant-goers were treated to

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.917
watching "The Sisterhood of the
Traveling Pants" decorate jeans.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.453
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my gosh.

00:00:54.453 --> 00:00:56.656
-Then the liquor came,
and then it got more exciting.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:58.091
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:01.193
-People got to watch us decorate
jeans inside of a restaurant.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:02.829
-What a good group.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.898
Hey, America, I want to talk
to you about -- we're five days

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.133
from Election Day,
and I just want to thank you.

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:12.105
You're doing so much great work
to help encourage people

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.775
to vote
and keeping people informed.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:19.579
I follow you on Instagram
and all the socials.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:23.216
But you do a thing
called America AF

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:26.819
where you answer
simple questions about voting.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:29.122
It's safe to watch-ish.
It's bleeped.

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:30.723
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.592
-What made you start doing this?

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:36.695
-Well, you know, back in 2018
when we were trying to

00:01:36.695 --> 00:01:39.298
get people out
for the midterm elections,

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.867
I realized that, like,
a lot of people didn't even know

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:44.203
what the midterm elections
were or, like, what the point

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:45.404
of the midterm elections are.

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:47.340
Why they're important,
why they matter.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.077
And I realized I had this tool
to just, like, speak directly to

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.446
at least the people
who followed me

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.414
on social media and tell them.

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:58.117
And so I just started
trying to explain things,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:01.921
and I remember asking people
like, "Why aren't you voting?

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:05.291
Like, just tell me.
I need to know. Like, why?"

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:09.528
And, you know, I'm expecting
these big ideological, you know,

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:12.565
reasons of like,
you know, "The system's rigged.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:13.699
Nothing's ever going to change."

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:15.969
And then the responses
were like,

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.371
"I don't have a car
to get to the polls."

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.707
Like, "I don't know
what I.D. I need."

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:23.476
"I don't know where to go
to get registered."

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.643
"No one's ever asked me --"

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:29.115
Like, all of these
super-simple problems

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.951
to solve with information,
with resources,

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:34.587
and I thought, you know,
"I'm going to do what I can do

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:37.689
with my platform to give
information to the people

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:39.091
who are following me."

00:02:39.091 --> 00:02:41.961
It feels like the thing
people come up against the most

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:44.530
is like,
"Does it really even matter?

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.031
Does my vote matter?"

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:49.102
You know, and I was lucky enough
to speak to this, like,

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:51.070
superhero election law attorney.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.905
Again, like, I'm a big nerd.

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:54.240
Like, that's what
gets me excited.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.875
I get to speak to, like,
an election law attorney,

00:02:56.875 --> 00:03:00.579
Marc Elias, and, you know,
I asked him like,

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:03.115
"Does every vote matter?"

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:06.653
And he just said,
"Oh, my God, does it matter."

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:10.122
Like, in 2018, we lost
a Senate race in Florida

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:13.359
by one tenth of 1%.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.328
Do you know what would be
happening right now

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.430
if there was another Democrat
in the Senate?

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.201
So one of the things people have
to remember is that, you know,

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:22.868
we're not just voting for
the president.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:26.172
You're voting for
senators and congressmen

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:29.275
and district attorneys
and sheriffs,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:33.212
people who make such a massive
impact in your community

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:35.948
and arguably have just as much

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:37.750
if not more impact
than the president.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.320
So showing up and voting
in those local races

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.391
often comes down to
just tens of votes.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:48.127
And so it so matters
that we show up.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:51.964
-I want to talk about your new
initiative called She Se Puede.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:54.400
-Good pronunciation, I love it.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.235
-Not bad? Thank you.
-Not bad.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:57.470
-I appreciate that.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.839
Can you explain how this started
and what it is?

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:03.476
-Yeah. She Se Puede is a new
online platform that I launched

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:07.413
with my dear friend Eva Longoria
and also a number of

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.850
other amazing Latinas
across business and politics

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.186
and organizing and activism.

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:17.723
And it's a new lifestyle
platform for Latinas.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:21.227
It's a place for us to be
celebrated and to be empowered

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:25.164
in everything from, you know,
raising children, cooking meals,

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.566
our workouts,
asking for a raise,

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:30.102
and also our engagement
civically.

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.771
I mean, the numbers of Latinas

00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:35.207
are so often spoken about
in this country.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:37.076
But numbers don't equal power.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.711
Power equals power.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.680
And in order for us
to really make a difference

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.552
and to be reflected and
represented in this country,

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:51.657
we need to be empowered to use
our -- use the power

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:55.528
of our numbers to change the way
politics happen in this country,

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:57.530
to change the way
our communities are treated.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.465
And so the civic-action piece is

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.868
a really big part of
She Se Puede.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:05.738
But it's really about
celebrating and reflecting

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.840
every aspect of our lives.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.308
-Yeah, good for you.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:10.709
I want to talk about
"Superstore"

00:05:10.709 --> 00:05:13.312
because Season 6
premiered tonight.

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:17.116
And next week is
your character's final episode.

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.484
-That's right, yeah.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.887
-You were about to
film this episode in March,

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:24.490
and then the pandemic hit, so
you came back again this season.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.593
And you had to
basically say goodbye

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:30.362
to everyone
twice or three times?

00:05:30.362 --> 00:05:34.733
-Yeah, I had, like, my final
table read three times.

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:38.170
And it was --
it was just a mess.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.406
I mean, I, like, sobbed
like a baby the first time.

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.941
-Of course.
-I couldn't even get words out.

00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:45.010
And then the second time,
I didn't know it was going to

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.447
be my last table read,
and then --

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:50.316
so when the final day finally
came, I had nothing to give.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:52.184
I had, like, cried
all the tears.

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:53.986
I said goodbye.
-"Yeah, I'm exhausted.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:56.054
You know I love you."
-Yeah.

00:05:56.054 --> 00:05:59.058
-But I'm happy that
you got to come back

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.294
and finish it the right way.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.595
-Totally.

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:05.731
And I -- You know, I did
100 episodes of the show.

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:06.899
I love the show.

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.702
I'm so glad that it's going on

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.270
and that the fans are going to

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.071
continue to watch it
and love it.

00:06:13.071 --> 00:06:15.741
And I'm always going to be a fan
of the show.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:19.412
And it was such an amazing
five years of my career,

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:22.181
and I'm always going to be
grateful for it.

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:23.682
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:23.682 --> 00:06:27.353
Here's America Ferrera
in "Superstore." Take a look.

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:30.622
-And Zephra is adamant that we
follow the CDC recommendation of

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:34.227
washing your hands
for a full 20 seconds.

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:35.361
-20 seconds?

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.096
That's like literally forever.

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:39.498
-It's about the length of
two "Happy Birthdays."

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:41.167
So just sing that
and you'll be fine.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.468
-Yeah, but whose name
do we insert?

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:43.702
I have a friend named Ty

00:06:43.702 --> 00:06:45.605
and I have a friend named
Alexandria Genevieve.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:47.473
Those are two wildly
different timeframes.

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:49.609
-Just say the name Emma.

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:50.942
-Oh, God.

00:06:50.942 --> 00:06:52.778
Is this your way of making us
sing "Happy Birthday"

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:54.413
to Emma because all of
her friends ditched her

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:55.647
on her birthday this year?

00:06:55.647 --> 00:06:57.349
-No, that didn't happen.

00:06:57.349 --> 00:06:59.284
Emma is very popular,

00:06:59.284 --> 00:07:02.254
and she's dating a very cute boy
name Braden.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.556
Very cute.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.090
I don't mean cute
like I think he's hot

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.992
because I'm an adult
and that would be weird.

00:07:06.992 --> 00:07:09.261
Whatever. Just wash your hands.
There's a virus.

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:10.629
-America Ferrera.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:14.733
"Superstore" airs Thursday
nights at 8:00 p.m. on NBC.

00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:18.204
And make sure you check out
shesepuede.org.

